                "entry_id": "21882b0b-74f1-35d9-a618-a3379031ef6e",
                "word": "uxorious",
                "pos": "adj",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "21ad04c3-c288-464d-b7be-74bd8acfe9f0",
                        "entry_id": "21882b0b-74f1-35d9-a618-a3379031ef6e",
                        "tags": [
                            "comparative"
                        ],
                        "form": "more uxorious"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "65f106b1-76e5-4541-9636-23e3f5190d95",
                        "entry_id": "21882b0b-74f1-35d9-a618-a3379031ef6e",
                        "tags": [
                            "superlative"
                        ],
                        "form": "most uxorious"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                        "entry_id": "21882b0b-74f1-35d9-a618-a3379031ef6e",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Very devoted and possibly submissive to one's wife; showing excessive uxorial devotion"
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "23d2cd9e-800c-4604-a99f-ac40cff05088",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "With theſe in troop\nCame Aſtoreth, whom the Phœnicians call'd\nAſtarte, Queen of Heav'n, with creſcent Horns;\nTo whoſ,e bright Image nightly by the Moon\nSidonian Virgins paid their Vows and Songs,\nIn Sion alſo not unſung, where ſtood\nHer Temple on th' offensive Mountain, built\nBy that uxorious King, whoſe heart though large,\nBeguil'd by fair Idolatreſſes, fell\nTo Idols foul."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "cbfefdea-de47-496a-a286-06300dcbd526",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "In truth, Richard was a fond—almost an uxorious husband."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "b79f9c61-cf01-4362-a6cd-c2f55a1a4653",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "Mr. Watkins Tottle was a rather uncommon compound of strong uxorious inclinations, and an unparalleled degree of anti-connubial timidity."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "208a6e6c-b95d-4a2e-9f61-ae5d5ea0eb34",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "The king, who was a most uxorious husband, at first ridiculed the notion, but at length consented, as he did to everything else on which her majesty had set her heart."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "673cbecb-2a72-47b9-8589-0066aab5b642",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "People smiled a little when Sir Andrew Ffoulkes' name was mentioned, some called him effeminate, others uxorious, his fond attachment for his pretty little wife was thought to pass the bounds of decorum."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "99e84b35-6b74-41f8-95c0-e2e01ec11f1d",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "But Daylight was not unduly uxorious. He lived his man's life just as she lived her woman's life."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "79be165a-3052-4462-af3b-dbf7be7e1c57",
                                "sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                                "text": "She was the cross her cuckolded, incompatible husband had to bear, and he was—beneath those fantastic uniforms—the pathetic, uxorious human aggregate of love and good intentions, which, quite frankly, bored her most of the time."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "67325d5b-a750-41c5-afb6-65ced647735c",
                        "source_entry_id": "21882b0b-74f1-35d9-a618-a3379031ef6e",
                        "source_sense_id": "f03af960-32ec-308d-a585-c109b6b1efca",
                        "source_sense_description": "Very devoted and/or submissive to one's wife",
                        "target_lang_word": "uxorieux",
                        "target_lang_code": "fr",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "uxorieux",
                                "target_lang_code": "fr",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "af432dc1-aea4-343a-873a-9f500b34a64c",
                                        "entry_id": "8437a9d3-3a44-33fe-973a-b79112cfa7b4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Se dit d’un époux qui se laisse gouverner par sa femme ^([1]), qui est d’une nature faible et de sentiments très développés pour son épouse."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "rare"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "156094d0-5f60-4717-ab6b-c5c6fcce4798",
                                                "sense_id": "af432dc1-aea4-343a-873a-9f500b34a64c",
                                                "text": "Uxorieux, comme le disait autrefois l’Église, c’est-à-dire uni successivement à ses deux femmes par un lien surtout charnel, Monsieur de Sacy semblait céder davantage à la seconde dans les petites choses, mais gardait autour de lui cette zone désertique de froideur sèche, de politesse acariâtre dans laquelle sans doute il se sentait libre."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "222b106f-7033-49e7-bb0e-ba6c39e64024",
                                                "sense_id": "af432dc1-aea4-343a-873a-9f500b34a64c",
                                                "text": "Un romain se devait de n’être pas uxorieux, soit de ne pas être l’amant de sa femme."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "780d19a4-1c95-3834-b088-368fb5ca60c8",
                                        "entry_id": "8437a9d3-3a44-33fe-973a-b79112cfa7b4",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Se dit d’un homme dont l’affection pervertie s’est égarée en se reportant sur sa propre femme ^([2])."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "Par plaisanterie"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
